# CODEOWNERS — security-critical paths in Horus
#
# Every path below is verified to exist. The previous revision listed seven files that never
# existed (src/kernel/capability.h, paging.h, syscall.h, scheduler.h, crypto.h,
# docs/SECURITY.md), whose rules were therefore dead, while omitting the files that actually
# contain the IPC authorisation logic — see finding [I-8] in docs/AUDIT-2026-07-27.md.
#
# NOTE: these rules only bind if branch protection sets `require_code_owner_review: true`.
# It is currently false, so this file is advisory. Fixing that is roadmap item 4.1.
#
# See CONTRIBUTING.md for the invariant-statement requirement on these paths.

# ---- Capability system: the core of the security model ----------------------
rust/src/capability.rs          @pharanyx-labs
rust/src/memory.rs              @pharanyx-labs
src/kernel/capability.c         @pharanyx-labs
# untyped.c decides who may create a kernel object and when one is destroyed --
# the authority-to-allocate half of the capability model (finding [I-7]).
src/kernel/untyped.c            @pharanyx-labs
# The conformance suite IS the evidence for the capability claims; weakening a
# check there is as security-relevant as weakening the kernel it tests.
userspace/captest.c             @pharanyx-labs

# ---- Syscall dispatch and authorisation -------------------------------------
# All the syscall*.c files, not just the dispatcher: syscall_ipc.c holds the IPC
# authorisation path, syscall_hw.c the device-delegation gates, syscall_fs.c the
# object-store gates.
src/kernel/syscall.c            @pharanyx-labs
src/kernel/syscall_ipc.c        @pharanyx-labs
src/kernel/syscall_fs.c         @pharanyx-labs
src/kernel/syscall_hw.c         @pharanyx-labs
# pci.c is what syscall_hw.c's gates ASK. It decides what a CAP_IO_DEVICE names
# and which frames, ports and interrupt lines that device declares, so a mistake
# here widens hardware authority without touching a syscall (S43).
src/kernel/pci.c                @pharanyx-labs
# iommu.c decides what a DEVICE may reach. A mistake here is a device writing
# where no capability said it could, performed by hardware, with no software on
# the path to catch it (S45).
src/kernel/iommu.c              @pharanyx-labs
# The device-capability conformance suite, for the same reason captest.c is
# owned: it is the evidence for S43, and every check in it is a refusal.
userspace/devcaptest.c          @pharanyx-labs
# netd holds a bus-mastering device capability, which on an IOMMU-less machine is
# authority over all of physical memory. It is the first program in this tree for
# which that is true, and a change to what it programs into a descriptor is a
# change to where hardware writes (S44).
userspace/netd.c                @pharanyx-labs
src/kernel/syscall_internal.h   @pharanyx-labs
include/syscall.h               @pharanyx-labs

